 \section{Conclusions}
 The metric-derived elliptic shift provides a concrete spherical gauge construction that preserves independently evolved Z4 fields. Its $g=1$ reduction admits efficient stage solves, while analytic cancellation, positive-variable charts, and explicit inner-end limits make difficult regularity and conditioning issues accessible to independent tests. The historical positive evidence comprises short-time finite-pulse field convergence and coupled subcritical mass--flux convergence. The telegrapher follow-up adds a full-domain three-grid study of the inward lapse layer and stage-integrated gauge characteristics through $t=10$, passing the declared spatial, boundary, positivity, and lapse-gradient checks for the fixed candidate. The finest peak Hamiltonian RMS and interior pointwise error are $1.53\times10^{-8}$ and $2.29\times10^{-5}$, respectively, with a maximum matched gradient discrepancy of $0.103\%$. These results support spatial resolution of the finite-duration front in this gauge; the narrow inner lapse bump is still present. The specialized CMC-source control supplies a useful long-lived geometric check.
 
-The same evidence prevents a stronger stability claim. The low-resolution continuation targeting $t=500$ has a nearly steady late exterior constraint budget and accurate apparent-horizon mass through the interim snapshot at $t=20$ (Sec.~\ref{sec:tele-long}). Its large puncture-interior errors are excluded from this revised budget, and puncture mass estimates are not acceptance quantities. This promising exterior behavior still requires the requested longer evolution and an exterior convergence study. Fine-grid constraints encounter arithmetic limits; coarse endpoint convergence can fail transiently; and the historical strong-field and collapse experiments remain unacceptable. The telegrapher follow-up addresses a different gauge prescription, but the unrun independent quadrature, dissipation-amplitude, and adjacent-parameter controls prevent its promotion to a robustly validated scheme. The remaining scientific requirements include completing the independent time/elliptic/dissipation and adjacent-parameter studies for the selected full-domain gauge, establishing longer-duration strong-field behavior, a demonstrated wormhole-to-trumpet limit, and successful coupled collapse followed by radiation extraction over the required durations. The present formulation is an experimentally characterized candidate with preserved failures, rather than a completed nonlinear hyperboloidal black-hole scheme.
